<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Second Spanish Republic</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1931">1931</a>–<a href="/years/1939">1939</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Spanish Republic, Segunda República Española</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Second Spanish Republic was the democratic government of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> from 1931 to 1939, proclaimed on 14 April 1931 after the deposition of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-xiii">Alfonso XIII</a>. Under Prime Minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuel-azana">Manuel Azaña</a> religious orders lost control of schools, a moderate agrarian reform was carried out and Catalonia gained home rule. After the CEDA won the 1933 election, clashes between left and right grew, and an uprising in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asturias">Asturias</a> in 1934 was crushed. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/popular-front-chile">Popular Front</a> won in February 1936, and in July an army uprising began the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-civil-war">Spanish Civil War</a>, in which an estimated half a million people died. The Republic surrendered to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-franco">Francisco Franco</a>'s Nationalists on 1 April 1939.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1935 the Falangists wanted a stirring song of their own to answer the &quot;Himno de Riego&quot;, the official anthem of the <b>Second Spanish Republic</b>, and &quot;A las Barricadas&quot;, a very popular anarchist song.</blockquote><a href="/songs/cara-al-sol">Cara al Sol</a></li><li><blockquote>By the time of the <b>Second Spanish Republic</b> (1931–1939) it had regained its popularity and became the chief musical symbol of the government.</blockquote><a href="/songs/himno-de-riego">Himno de Riego</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Second Spanish Republic</b> used the Himno de Riego instead.</blockquote><a href="/songs/marcha-real">Marcha Real</a></li></ul></section>
